  "id": "DrugBank_DB01210 - MONDO_5338",
  "verbalization": "Levobunolol is associated with Open-angle Glaucoma through a chain of indications that link it to ocular hypertension, which in turn is linked to specific medications. The evidence suggests that Levobunolol may exert a role in the pathogenesis of Open-angle Glaucoma via its effects on ocular hypertension, potentially through mechanisms that influence intraocular pressure or vascular function. The presence of multiple pathways—each involving Levobunolol and a different medication (Latanoprostene Bunod, Befunolol, and Dorzolamide)—indicates that Levobunolol may interact with ocular physiology in a way that contributes to the development of open-angle glaucoma. These findings suggest a possible role for Levobunolol in the management or pathogenesis of this condition, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of considering ocular hypertension as a potential intermediate step in the relationship between Levobunolol and Open-angle Glaucoma.",
